<h2 id="what">What it does, and doesn't</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Archive access.</strong> It lists what an archive holds, finds the days that were never fetched, and downloads a day into a local cache, where Databento's own <code>databento</code> package opens it. The archive can be in S3 or a folder. It follows the <a href="docs/layout">data layout</a>'s rules, so a day never fetched is never taken for a day without trading.</li>
    <li><strong>Tapes.</strong> It reads S1, Trade and L1 tapes into numpy structured arrays or pandas DataFrames, checking each tape's checksums as C# does.</li>
    <li><strong>Monte Carlo runs.</strong> It deals sessions with the same seeded shuffle as C#, so a seed gives the same sessions in both languages, and stitches them end to end with the same offsets.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    It does <strong>not</strong> rebuild order books, make bars, back-adjust or write NinjaTrader files. For those, run
    <a href="docs/dbnfetch#export"><code>dbnf export</code></a> and read its Parquet. One engine, in C#, keeps every
    language's numbers the same.
</p>

<h2 id="install">Install</h2>
<p>
    The package is <code>dbnsuite</code>, for Python 3.10 or later, in
    <a href="@Links.PythonClient">Clients/python</a>. It isn't on PyPI yet, so install it from a clone of the
    repository:
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="From the repository's folder" Text="@Install" />
<p>
    <code>[dbn]</code> adds Databento's <code>databento</code> package, for opening raw days, and <code>[pandas]</code>
    adds pandas. Without them it needs only boto3, numpy, xxhash and zstandard.
</p>

<h2 id="archive">Read an archive</h2>
<p>
    <code>Archive</code> takes the archive's S3 address and a read-only key, or a folder, such as one
    <code>dbnf pull --out</code> filled. <code>Archive.from_env()</code> reads the same <code>DBNF_*</code> variables
    as <code>dbnf</code>. Days are copied into a cache folder, from S3 or a folder archive alike, so reading a day
    twice fetches it once.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Python" Text="@Archive" />
<p>
    <code>archive.days(schema, root)</code> lists one series' days, each with its stored object, and
    <code>archive.path(...)</code> gives a cached day's local file without opening it. A day never fetched raises
    <code>MissingDay</code>, and a day with no data gives <code>None</code>. Never put a secret in source: read it
    from the environment.
</p>
<p>
    The cache is <code>DBNSUITE_CACHE</code> if that's set, else <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\dbnsuite\cache</code> on
    Windows, <code>~/Library/Caches/dbnsuite</code> on macOS and <code>~/.cache/dbnsuite</code> (or
    <code>$XDG_CACHE_HOME/dbnsuite</code>) on Linux; <code>cache=</code> picks another. It's separate from
    <code>dbnf</code>'s.
</p>

<h2 id="tapes">Tapes</h2>
<CodeBlock Title="Python" Text="@Tapes" />
<p>
    Times are microseconds since the UNIX epoch. Prices are whole ticks; multiply by
    <code>tape.header.price_scale</code> for the price. Every field keeps its last value until an event changes it, as
    a C# cursor does. The decoder is plain Python, at about a second per hundred thousand events, so an L1 day can take
    a while. See <a href="docs/dbntape">DbnTape</a> for what each kind holds.
</p>

<h2 id="sessions">Monte Carlo sessions</h2>
<p>
    <code>SessionSampler</code> deals a run's sessions: warm-up sessions drawn at random, then every day once in a
    random order, or with <code>sampling="bootstrap"</code>, drawn with replacement. <code>SessionStream</code> loads
    each session's tape and works out the offset that stitches it onto the one before.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Python" Text="@Sessions" />
<p>
    <code>sampler.fingerprint</code> is a hash of the days. Keep it with a seed, because the same seed deals
    differently once new tapes arrive.
</p>

<h2 id="tests">Tests</h2>
<CodeBlock Title="From the repository's folder" Text="@Tests" />
<p>
    The examples on this page are in <a href="@Links.PythonExamples">Clients/python/examples</a>, and the tests run
    each one on the fixtures and check this page and the README still quote them. The fixtures are synthetic:
    generated prices, not market data. C# writes them and checks them in
    <code>PythonFixturesTests</code>, so a change to the tape format or the sampler that breaks Python fails the C#
    build too. The <a href="@Links.Contributing">contributing guide</a> covers clients in other languages.
</p>

